      "card_ids": ["C30"],
      "claim": "Bumetanide, a structurally related sulfamoylbenzoic acid loop diuretic, has absolute oral bioavailability 0.72-0.96 in normal volunteers, supporting high F for the query's sulfamoylbenzoic acid scaffold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C09"],
      "claim": "Hydrochlorothiazide, a sulfonamide diuretic sharing the sulfamoyl and chloroaromatic features, shows oral bioavailability of 60-80% in healthy volunteers, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C39"],
      "claim": "Tolfenamic acid, a carboxylic acid-containing anthranilic acid drug, has absolute oral bioavailability averaging 60% in healthy volunteers, supporting high F for the carboxylate-containing query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C45"],
      "claim": "Azosemide, a close analogue with the same sulfamoylchloroaminobenzene core but a tetrazole replacing the carboxylate, has low oral bioavailability of 10-19% in humans, providing a contradictory low-F data point.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is furosemide. Multiple structurally related sulfonamide/anthranilic acid drugs (bumetanide, hydrochlorothiazide, tolfenamic acid) have human oral bioavailability well above 20%, and furosemide itself is known to have F around 60%. The low-F azosemide analogue is less transferable because its tetrazole replaces the carboxylate. Prediction: high.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct furosemide parent bioavailability record is present; C06 reports the acyl glucuronide metabolite, not the parent drug.",
    "The closest low-F analogue (azosemide) differs by tetrazole-for-carboxylate and thiophene-for-furan substitutions, so its low F may not transfer to the query.",
    "No query-specific pharmacokinetic data under fasted conditions are provided."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

